 The Whale, 1st American edition, New York, N.Y.: Harper & Brothers; London: Richard Bentley, →OCLC, page 143:"}], "synonyms": [], "antonyms": [], "translations": [], "subsenses": []}], "synonyms": [], "antonyms": []}, {"language": {"code": "en", "name": "English"}, "partOfSpeech": "verb", "pronunciations": [{"type": "ipa", "text": "/mʌm/", "tags": []}], "forms": [{"word": "mums", "tags": ["present", "singular", "third-person"]}, {"word": "mumming", "tags": ["participle", "present"]}, {"word": "mummed", "tags": ["participle", "past"]}, {"word": "mummed", "tags": ["past"]}, {"word": "mumm", "tags": ["alternative", "archaic"]}], "senses": [{"definition": "To act in a pantomime or dumb show.", "tags": [], "examples": [], "quotes": [], "synonyms": [], "antonyms": [], "translations": [], "subsenses": []}, {"definition": "(transitive, UK, slang, obsolete) To silence.", "tags": ["UK", "obsolete", "slang", "transitive"], "examples": [], "quotes": [{"text": "\"Well, Sal, you mum your dubber pretty generally, but when you do slacken your glib you may as well do it civilly.\"", "reference": "1857, Hidden Links; Or the Schoolfellows. A Tale, volume 2, page 233:"}], "synonyms": [], "antonyms": [], "translations": [], "subsenses": []}], "synonyms": [], "antonyms": []}, {"language": {"code": "en", "name": "English"}, "partOfSpeech": "noun", "pronunciations": [{"type": "ipa", "text": "/mʌm/", "tags": []}], "forms": [{"word": "mumm", "tags": ["alternative", "archaic"]}], "senses": [{"definition": "(obsolete) Silence.", "tags": ["obsolete", "uncountable"], "examples": [], "quotes": [{"text": "Entrust it under solemn Vows / Of Mum and Silence,[…]", "reference": "1677 (indicated as 1678), [Samuel Butler], “[The Third Part of Hudibras]. Canto II.”, in Hudibras. The Third and Last Part. […], London: […] Robert Horne, […], published 1679, →OCLC; republished in A[lfred] R[ayney] Waller, editor, Hudibras: Written in the Time of the Late Wars, Cambridge, Cambridgeshire: University Press, 1905, →OCLC, page 277:"}], "synonyms": [], "antonyms": [], "translations": [], "subsenses": []}], "synonyms": [], "antonyms": []}, {"language": {"code": "en", "name": "English"}, "partOfSpeech": "noun", "pronunciations": [{"type": "ipa", "text": "/mʌm/", "tags": []}], "forms": [], "senses": [{"definition": "A type of strong beer, originally made in Brunswick, Germany.", "tags": ["uncountable"], "examples": [], "quotes": [{"text": "The clamorous crowd is hushed with mugs of mum.", "reference": "1728, [Alexander Pope], “(please specify the page)”, in The Dunciad. An Heroic Poem. […], Dublin; London: […] A. Dodd, →OCLC:"}], "synonyms": [], "antonyms": [], "translations": [], "subsenses": []}], "synonyms": [], "antonyms": []}, {"language": {"code": "en", "name": "English"}, "partOfSpeech": "noun", "pronunciations": [{"type": "ipa", "text": "/mʌm/", "tags": []}], "forms": [{"word": "mums", "tags": ["plural"]}], "senses": [{"definition": "(informal, dated) Alternative spelling of ma'am.", "tags": ["alt of", "alternative", "dated", "informal"], "examples": [], "quotes": [{"text": "“Wy, mum,” said Mr. Weller, “I don’t think you’ll see a many sich, and that’s the truth. But if my son Samivel vould give me my vay, mum, and only dis-pense vith his—might I wenter to say the vurd?” / “What word Mr. Weller?” said the housekeeper, blushing slightly. / “Petticuts, mum,” returned that gentleman, laying his hand upon the garments of his grandson. “If my son Samivel, mum, vould only dis-pense vith these here, you’d see such a alteration in his appearance, as the imagination can’t depicter.”", "reference": "1840 April – 1841 November, Charles Dickens, “Chapter the Eighth”, in The Old Curiosity Shop. A Tale. […], volume I, London: Chapman and Hall, […], published 1841, →OCLC, page 130:"}, {"text": "[H]e axed if wer stock o' coals was nearly done. I telled him it was, an' we was ill set to get more—but you know mum I didn't think o' him helping us—but howsever, he sent us a sack o' coals next day; […]", "reference": "1847 December, Acton Bell [pseudonym; Anne Brontë], “The Cottagers”, in Agnes Grey. […], London: Thomas Cautley Newby, […], →OCLC, page 176:"}, {"text": "\"A telegruff message, mum, for Mr. William,\" said the maid, looking at her mistress with eyes opened wide, as she handed the important bit of paper to her master.", "reference": "1865 May 15 – 1866 January 1, Anthony Trollope, “William Belton Does Not Go Out Hunting”, in The Belton Estate. […], volume II, London: Chapman and Hall, […], published December 1865 (indicated as 1866), →OCLC, pages 220–221:"}, {"text": "Then she took off the hank and looked me straight in the face, but very pleasant, and says: / “Come, now—what’s your real name? / “Wh-what, mum?” / “What’ your real name? Is it Bill, or Tom, or Bob?—or what is it?”", "reference": "1884 December 10, Mark Twain [pseudonym; Samuel Langhorne Clemens], chapter XI, in The Adventures of Huckleberry Finn: (Tom Sawyer’s Comrade) […], London: Chatto & Windus, […], →OCLC, page 93:"}], "synonyms": [], "antonyms": [], "translations": [], "subsenses": []}], "synonyms": [], "antonyms": []}], "source": {"url": "https://en.wiktionary.org/wiki/mum", "license": {"name": "CC BY-SA 4.0", "url": "https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by-sa/4.0/"}}}}